$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186420915 "![](images/f4/f413fd04759cdb02118b320fea2f678712681a57f5b08e1bcb57a6843c0c80f0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [17592186420916 "![](images/9d/9da6bdb9ab5f93d0b0d11dfd60ab2b647380a94ce337c2314ec3504cabafa889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [17592186420917 "![](images/57/575463a6fbe18d3f9b805e1a9c3d8e52bedd84a8db3a3c90d87381b8cb7abf74.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [17592186421470 "![](images/95/958965484cd271c5eb7ccfc8bf913386e57b9b733719885d232e562063969ca8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [17592186421783 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2uv⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186421784 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3uv,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-3u⟩.$*"] [17592186421785 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [17592186424178 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$"] [17592186424179 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants."] [17592186424180 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186424181 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*"] [17592186424182 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$"] [17592186424621 "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186424622 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186431183 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2).$"] [17592186431184 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above."] [17592186431185 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$"] [17592186431186 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(-t-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$"] [17592186431187 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-{z}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$"] [17592186431188 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1)$"] [17592186434830 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}xz,\\hspace{0.222em}yz⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186434831 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.$*"] [17592186436123 "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$"] [17592186436124 "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,-3,2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$"] [17592186436125 "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$"] [17592186436126 "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$"] [17592186436127 "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186436128 "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$"] [17592186436129 "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=\n$$"] [17592186436130 "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186437020 "Given $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [17592186437021 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z